{:edb/db-before-t 1369 :edb/db-after-t 1370 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534682 50 #inst "2026-10-08T04:04:27.713Z" 13194139534682 true] [17592186105633 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303176 13194139534682 true] [17592186105633 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303190 13194139534682 true] [17592186105633 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303214 13194139534682 true] [17592186105634 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303162 13194139534682 true] [17592186105634 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303169 13194139534682 true] [17592186105634 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303200 13194139534682 true] [17592186105635 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303183 13194139534682 true] [17592186105635 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303193 13194139534682 true] [17592186105635 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303196 13194139534682 true] [17592186105635 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303207 13194139534682 true] [17592186303162 1017 #edb/ref 17592186303163 13194139534682 true] [17592186303162 1089 "q-113762" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303162 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534682 true] [17592186303162 1100 "Given the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-3x+5,$ which of the following statements are true?" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303162 1113 #uuid "d9a92603-1c58-5381-9a97-44ce967ac9d7" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303162 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}+6x-3 \\\\  & =-3({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =-3(x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3(x-1)^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-3(0-1)^{2}=-3<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-3(2-1)^{2}=-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." 13194139534682 true] [17592186303163 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303164 13194139534682 true] [17592186303163 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303165 13194139534682 true] [17592186303163 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303166 13194139534682 true] [17592186303163 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303167 13194139534682 true] [17592186303163 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303168 13194139534682 true] [17592186303163 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534682 true] [17592186303163 1041 "selection" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303163 1104 #edb/ref 17592186303166 13194139534682 true] [17592186303163 1110 #uuid "3a3c5888-f08b-57a9-8445-91e772da6c66" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303164 1034 "There is a local minimum at $x=-1$" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303164 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534682 true] [17592186303164 1111 #uuid "a29e3487-e0d5-5619-942e-66e55b9015a7" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303165 1034 "There is a local minimum at $x=1$" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303165 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534682 true] [17592186303165 1111 #uuid "ee3d28cc-1ab6-5f34-a650-78711ffa2294" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303166 1034 "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303166 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534682 true] [17592186303166 1111 #uuid "06c4c9eb-f86e-5536-b095-bb6cd4e29bdb" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303167 1034 "There is a local maximum at $x=-1$" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303167 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534682 true] [17592186303167 1111 #uuid "a733457a-c441-5230-925d-a12a204900b9" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303168 1034 "There is a local maximum at $x=1$" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303168 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534682 true] [17592186303168 1111 #uuid "6741f1d8-0e76-5d19-ab0b-7847d8c593c2" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303169 1017 #edb/ref 17592186303170 13194139534682 true] [17592186303169 1089 "q-113768" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303169 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534682 true] [17592186303169 1100 "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{{x}^{3}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303169 1113 #uuid "d734b4ff-5976-5354-96f9-b1ca59071265" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303169 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(2x-3){x}^{3}-3({x}^{2}-3x+3){x}^{2}}{{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-3{x}^{2}+9x-9}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+6x-9}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nSo, $x=3$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $(0,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=1,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{(1-3)^{2}}{{1}^{4}}=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =-\\frac{(4-3)^{2}}{{4}^{4}}=-\\frac{1}{256}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=3.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=3$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema on $(0,\\infty ).$" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303171 13194139534682 true] [17592186303170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303172 13194139534682 true] [17592186303170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303173 13194139534682 true] [17592186303170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303174 13194139534682 true] [17592186303170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303175 13194139534682 true] [17592186303170 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534682 true] [17592186303170 1041 "selection" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303170 1104 #edb/ref 17592186303174 13194139534682 true] [17592186303170 1110 #uuid "82cece45-c39d-5eb8-a9be-12814d5d653d" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303171 1034 "There is a local minimum at $x=-3$" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534682 true] [17592186303171 1111 #uuid "acc58338-ffd3-5655-9cbe-3d640666a7f8" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303172 1034 "There is a local maximum at $x=-3$" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303172 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534682 true] [17592186303172 1111 #uuid "5b33665a-1d58-5ff6-a744-5babee0c2b71" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303173 1034 "There is a local maximum at $x=3$" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303173 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534682 true] [17592186303173 1111 #uuid "a93f83b3-d67e-563b-be91-54bbed67199f" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303174 1034 "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303174 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534682 true] [17592186303174 1111 #uuid "ddd8d1bb-a88c-5f25-a74e-013aa6c6fd68" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303175 1034 "There is a local minimum at $x=3$" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303175 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534682 true] [17592186303175 1111 #uuid "39acc041-7f03-58cc-8d8b-a10f6b78c616" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303176 1017 #edb/ref 17592186303177 13194139534682 true] [17592186303176 1089 "q-21467" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303176 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534682 true] [17592186303176 1100 "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-12x+6,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-2$ is a local maximum\n- $x=1$ is a local minimum\n- $x=1$ is a local maximum" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303176 1113 #uuid "f13d0a0f-3535-547f-bf2f-0daa5e0b2ddb" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303176 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+6x-12 \\\\  & =6({x}^{2}+x-2) \\\\  & =6(x+2)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(x+2)(x-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =6(-3+2)(-3-1)=24>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6(0+2)(0-1)=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6(2+2)(2-1)=24>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303178 13194139534682 true] [17592186303177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303179 13194139534682 true] [17592186303177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303180 13194139534682 true] [17592186303177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303181 13194139534682 true] [17592186303177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303182 13194139534682 true] [17592186303177 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534682 true] [17592186303177 1041 "selection" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303177 1104 #edb/ref 17592186303181 13194139534682 true] [17592186303177 1110 #uuid "39d8d08d-8ae7-56e2-9446-ea879b820394" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303178 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534682 true] [17592186303178 1111 #uuid "1dbd6480-4929-5949-a0b1-a153881b8a28" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303179 1034 "I only" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303179 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534682 true] [17592186303179 1111 #uuid "827e9847-0070-52a6-95e1-7794ccecf414" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303180 1034 "III only" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303180 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534682 true] [17592186303180 1111 #uuid "b7b76953-7cdd-5176-9bad-4c05b0ca7cb3" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303181 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534682 true] [17592186303181 1111 #uuid "66a03397-9f2f-56bd-8d80-5e198dc9d668" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303182 1034 "II only" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303182 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534682 true] [17592186303182 1111 #uuid "99b1c3a7-5dfa-54d7-b84a-2651e9b4aea1" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303183 1017 #edb/ref 17592186303184 13194139534682 true] [17592186303183 1089 "q-22504" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303183 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534682 true] [17592186303183 1100 "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local maximum?" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303183 1113 #uuid "4f2f830a-8fd4-5def-9efa-5e26534df70d" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303183 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot {x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 2x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & ={e}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x={e}^{1/2}$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x={e}^{1/3},e$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}({e}^{1/3}) & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{1/3}}{{({e}^{1/3})}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{3e}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e}{{e}^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,{e}^{1/2})$ | ${e}^{1/2}$ | $({e}^{1/2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | ${e}^{1/3}$ |  | $e$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x={e}^{1/2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x={e}^{1/2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x={e}^{1/2}.$" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303184 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303185 13194139534682 true] [17592186303184 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303186 13194139534682 true] [17592186303184 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303187 13194139534682 true] [17592186303184 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303188 13194139534682 true] [17592186303184 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303189 13194139534682 true] [17592186303184 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534682 true] [17592186303184 1041 "selection" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303184 1104 #edb/ref 17592186303186 13194139534682 true] [17592186303184 1110 #uuid "ef257078-8c2b-5897-9e3a-55eb8c36d31a" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303185 1034 "{e}^{2/3}" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303185 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534682 true] [17592186303185 1111 #uuid "147d98de-aec3-5443-83a9-b7444200833f" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303186 1034 "{e}^{1/2}" 13194139534682 true] [17592186303186 1103 #edb/ref 1759218604545